    {
      "id": "be-s8-production-functions-c1",
      "heading": "What a production function says",
      "explanation_md": "A **production function** links inputs to output: **Q = F(K,L)**. Q is the quantity of output, **K** is capital, **L** is labour, and F is the functional form that turns inputs into output.\n\nIt gives the **maximum** output a firm can produce with K units of capital and L units of labour. Producing less than that is wasting inputs.\n\nThe session splits decisions into the **short run**, where some inputs are **fixed**, and the **long run**, where inputs are **variable**.",
      "example_ids": [],
      "key_terms": [
        {
          "term": "Production function",
          "meaning": "Q = F(K,L): the maximum output from K units of capital and L units of labour",
          "source_ref": "be--session-8#3"
        },
        {
          "term": "Fixed input",
          "meaning": "An input the firm cannot change in the short run",
          "source_ref": "be--session-8#3"
        }
      ],
      "exam_line": "Q = F(K,L) is the maximum output that K units of capital and L units of labour can produce.",
      "clarification": null,
